\n- Continue until the sum exceeds the original number.\n- Check if the original number is in the sequence.\n\nBut wait, the sequence starts with the digits. For example, if the number is 197, the sequence starts with 1, 9, 7. Then the next term is 1+9+7=17, and so on. So the sequence is generated until the sum is larger than the original number. Then check if the original number is in the sequence.\n\nBut how to handle the sequence generation? Let's think of the steps:\n\nInitialize the sequence with the digits of n. Then, for each step, compute the sum of all elements in the sequence (excluding the last one?), or include the last one? Wait, the sequence is generated by taking the sum of the previous terms. So for example, the first term is the first digit, the second term is the second digit, the third term is the third digit, and the fourth term is the sum of the first three, etc. So the sequence is generated as follows:\n\nsequence = [d1, d2, d3, ... dn]\nnext_term = sum(sequence)\nsequence.append(next_term)\nnext_term = sum(sequence)\nsequence.append(next_term)\nand so on.\n\nSo the process is: for each term after the first three, add the sum of all previous terms.\n\nBut for the number 197, the sequence starts as [1,9,7], then 1+9+7=17, then 9+7+17=33, then 7+17+33=57, then 17+33+57=107, then 33+57+107=197. So 197 is in the sequence.\n\nTherefore, the function needs to generate this sequence and check if the original number is present.\n\nNow, the challenge is to implement this in Python. Let's think about the code.\n\nFirst, the function is_num_keith(n):\n\ndef is_num_keith(n):\n    if n < 10:\n        # For single-digit numbers, they are Keith numbers if they are non-zero?\n        # Wait, the definition says that a Keith number is an integer that appears in the sequence. For example, 197 is a Keith number. For single-digit numbers, like 1, the sequence is [1], so 1 is in the sequence. So yes, single-digit numbers are Keith numbers.\n        return True\n    digits = list(map(int, str(n)))\n    sequence = digits.copy()\n    # Generate the sequence until the sum exceeds the original number\n    while True:\n        next_term = sum(sequence)\n        sequence.append(next_term)\n        if next_term == n:\n            return True\n        if next_term > n:\n            break\n    return False\n\nWait, but in the example of 197, the sequence is generated as [1,9,7], then 17, 33, 57, 107, 197. So the loop would check if next_term is n. But in the case of 197, the next_term after 107 is 197. So the loop would check if next_term is 197, which is true, and return True.\n\nBut wait, the code as written would check if next_term is equal to n, but the sequence is generated by adding the sum. So in the first iteration, the sequence is [1,9,7], next_term is 17. Then append 17, and check if 17 is 197? No. Then next_term is 9+7+17=33. Check if 33 is 197? No. Then 7+17+33=57, no. Then 17+33+57=107, no. Then 33+57+107=197. So next_term is 197, which is equal to n. So the code would return True.\n\nBut in the code above, the loop is:\n\nwhile True:\n    next_term = sum(sequence)\n    sequence.append(next_term)\n    if next_term == n:\n        return True\n    if next_term > n:\n        break\n\nBut in this case, the next_term is 197. So the code checks if next_term is n (197), which is true, returns True.\n\nBut the initial sequence is digits. So for 197, the initial sequence is [1,9,7], then next_term is 17, which is added. Then next_term is 33, added. Then 57, added. Then 107, added. Then 197, added. So the code would check if next_term (197) is equal to n (197), which is true. So returns True.\n\nBut wait, in the code, after appending next_term, the code checks if next_term is equal to n. So in this case, the next_term is 197, which is equal to n, so returns True.\n\nSo the code seems to work.\n\nBut wait, what about when the next_term is greater than n? For example, if the sequence is [1, 2, 3], n is 3. Then next_term is 6, which is