ue.\n- Otherwise, generate the next terms, and check again.\n\nBut how to do this efficiently?\n\nAnother approach is to generate the sequence, and for each term, check if it's equal to n. So the steps are:\n\nInitialize the sequence with the digits of n.\n\nThen, for each term in the sequence, check if it's equal to n. If any term is equal to n, return True.\n\nBut the sequence is generated by the sum of the previous terms. So the first term is the first digit, the second is the second digit, etc. So for example, if n is 197, the sequence starts as [1,9,7], and then each next term is the sum of all previous terms.\n\nSo the code needs to generate the sequence, and for each term in the sequence, check if it's equal to n. If any term is equal to n, return True. Otherwise, generate the next term and check again.\n\nBut the problem is that the sequence is generated step by step. So for example, the first term is the first digit, the second is the second, etc. So the code can generate the sequence, and check if n is in the sequence.\n\nBut the code needs to generate the sequence and check if n is in it. So the code can be modified as follows:\n\ndef is_num_keith(n):\n    if n < 10:\n        return True  # single-digit numbers are Keith numbers?\n    digits = list(map(int, str(n)))\n    sequence = digits.copy()\n    # Check if n is in the sequence\n    if n in sequence:\n        return True\n    # Generate the next terms and check\n    while True:\n        next_term = sum(sequence)\n        sequence.append(next_term)\n        if next_term == n:\n            return True\n        if next_term > n:\n            break\n    return False\n\nWait, but in the case of n=3, the sequence is [1,2,3]. The code checks if 3 is in the sequence, which it is, so returns True. That's correct.\n\nIn the case of n=197, the code checks if 197 is in the sequence [1,9,7], which it's not. Then generates next terms: 17, 33, 57, 107, 197. So the next_term is 197, which is equal to n. So returns True.\n\nBut what about when n is 2? The sequence is [1,2]. The code checks if 2 is in the sequence, which it is, returns True.\n\nAnother example: n=19. The digits are [1,9]. The sequence is [1,9]. Check if 19 is in the sequence. No. Generate next term: 1+9=10. Add 10. Now sequence is [1,9,10]. Check if 19 is in the sequence. No. Generate next term: 9+10=19. So next_term is 19, which is equal to n. Return True.\n\nSo this seems to work.\n\nBut what about when the original number is not in the sequence, but becomes part of the sequence after some steps?\n\nFor example, n=23. The sequence starts as [2,3]. Check if 23 is in the sequence. No. Generate next term: 2+3=5. Add 5. Sequence is [2,3,5]. Next term is 3+5=8. Add 8. Next term is 5+8=13. Add 13. Next term is 8+13=21. Add 21. Next term is 13+21=34. Add 34. Next term is 21+34=55. Add 55. Next term is 34+55=89. Add 89. Next term is 55+89=144. Add 1"
