rehension_score += list_comps\n\nSimilarly for dict and set comprehensions.\n\nThen, check for functional functions.\n\nThen, count assignments.\n\nThen, calculate the score.\n\nBut all of this is done in a single expression.\n\nBut how to combine all this into a single line.\n\nThe user wants a single, declarative expression, so perhaps using a list comprehension with all these steps.\n\nBut it\'s complex. However, the user might be expecting a concise code that applies all these steps.\n\nAlternatively, the solution could be to write a function that returns the score as per the reward function.\n\nBut given the time constraints, perhaps the answer is to write a function that returns the score as per the steps in the reward function, using a list comprehension to apply all the steps.\n\nHowever, given the complexity, the solution would be to implement the reward function as a single, Pythonic expression.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the code would be:\n\nimport re\nimport signal\n\ndef reward(solution_str):\n    # Extract answer\n    ans = extract_answer(solution_str)\n    # Clean the code\n    cleaned_code = ans\n    cleaned_code = re.sub(r\'""".*?"""\', \'\', cleaned_code, flags=re.DOTALL)\n    cleaned_code = re.sub(r"\'\'\'.*?\'\'\'", \'\', cleaned_code, flags=re.DOTALL)\n    cleaned_code = re.sub(r\'"[^"]*"\', \'\', cleaned_code)\n    cleaned_code = re.sub(r"\'[^\']*\'", \'\', cleaned_code)\n    cleaned_code = re.sub(r\'#.*$\', \'\', cleaned_code, flags=re.MULTILINE)\n    # Count explicit loops\n    explicit_loops = 0\n    for line in cleaned_code.split(\'\n\'):\n        line = line.strip()\n        if re.match(r\'^\\s*(for\\s+\\w+.*?:|while\\s+.*?:)\', line):\n            explicit_loops += 1\n    # Count comprehensions\n    list_comps = len(re.findall(r\'\\[[^[\\]]*\x08for\x08[^[\\]]*\\]\', ans))\n    comprehension_score = list_comps\n    dict_comps = len(re.findall(r\'\\{[^{}]*\x08for\x08[^{}]*\\}\', ans))\n    set_comps = len(re.findall(r\'\\{[^{}:]*\x08f