m says to minimize character count and write the most performant code. So I need to make sure the code is as concise as possible.\n\nLet me think about the code. Let\'s start with the function definition.\n\ndef prime_num(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n    if n == 2:\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    for i in range(3, int(n**0.5) + 1, 2):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\nWait, but the problem says to not use any additional input parameters. Wait, the function is supposed to take an integer as input. So the function is called with one parameter, which is the integer. So the code is correct.\n\nBut the problem says "the function should not use any additional input parameters or return any other type of result." So the function returns True or False, which is correct.\n\nBut the code as written is concise. However, maybe there\'s a way to make it even shorter.\n\nWait, the code can be written in a more compact way. For example, using a lambda or a one-liner. But in Python, using a lambda might not be allowed here, but the problem says to write the most performant and terse code possible. Let\'s see.\n\nAlternatively, using a one-liner with a generator expression. But maybe not necessary. Let\'s check the code.\n\nAnother approach: The code can be written as a one-liner. For example:\n\ndef prime_num(n):\n    return n < 2 or (n == 2) or (n % 2 == 0 and not any(n % i == 0 for i in range(3, int(n**0.5)+1, 2)))\n\nWait, but this would check if n is even and then check for any divisors. But the code as written would have the \'and not any(...)\' part. However, the \'any\' function returns True if any element is True, so if there\'s a divisor, the \'any\' returns True, and the \'and not\' would make the entire expression False. So that\'s correct.\n\nBut the code is a bit longer. Let me compare the two versions.\n\nOriginal code:\n\ndef prime_num(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n    if